WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:01.794
(audience cheers)

00:00:01.794 --> 00:00:03.450
- You are, like every time you come here,

00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:04.700
you look so dapper.

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:06.420
- Thank you so much, you look great too.

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:08.460
- Well someone, a whole team
of people makes this happen.

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:09.690
I don't look like this in general.

00:00:09.690 --> 00:00:10.523
- Well, same.

00:00:10.523 --> 00:00:12.150
- No, I feel like you probably
look like this at Target.

00:00:12.150 --> 00:00:13.077
- Okay, I appreciate-
- Yeah, you do.

00:00:13.077 --> 00:00:15.120
- I appreciate your optimism

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:16.530
when it comes to how I look.

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:17.637
- You totally do, probably.
- Thank you.

00:00:17.637 --> 00:00:19.440
- But wait, so New Year's just happened.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:20.850
How do you celebrate New Year's?

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:22.879
- I have absolutely no
expectations for New Year's.

00:00:22.879 --> 00:00:23.712
(Kelly laughs)

00:00:23.712 --> 00:00:24.960
I have no lists.
- No bar.

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.750
- I have no, I call
them like, at the very,

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:29.310
because what is it, New Year resolutions?

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:31.590
- Yeah, do you do those?
- No, obviously not.

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:32.460
I've tried.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:35.310
Every year you try, and
every year you fail.

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:38.550
Not to be a fatalist, but
I'm just terrible at them.

00:00:38.550 --> 00:00:39.900
- I don't like to say fail.

00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.610
I like to say just not there yet.

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:42.443
- Right, right, right, right,

00:00:42.443 --> 00:00:43.276
working on it, we're working on it.

00:00:43.276 --> 00:00:44.370
- [Kelly] Working on it, yeah.

00:00:44.370 --> 00:00:46.710
- I don't love to work on
things, so I'm just gonna-

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:48.445
- I love working on things.
- You do?

00:00:48.445 --> 00:00:49.278
- Yes!

00:00:49.278 --> 00:00:50.820
- So you stick to a list?

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:51.777
- I will stick to the list,

00:00:51.777 --> 00:00:53.850
and it doesn't matter
if it takes a decade,

00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:55.603
but I'm still doing it, yeah.
- Wow, okay great.

00:00:55.603 --> 00:00:59.250
- I just like to have, I
like goals, I like, yeah.

00:00:59.250 --> 00:01:02.850
- Yeah, well I also now like goals,

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:05.430
and want to set them for myself.

00:01:05.430 --> 00:01:07.020
- I do, I don't know why.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:09.390
I think it gives me
purpose, like to go like,

00:01:09.390 --> 00:01:10.423
okay, this is the thing I'm gonna work on.

00:01:10.423 --> 00:01:12.360
- I did a thing last
year where I said, okay,

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.190
I was gonna walk every day,

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:16.860
and I was gonna add like
an extra five minutes

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:18.060
every time I walked.

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:18.893
- Okay.

00:01:18.893 --> 00:01:20.523
- Yeah, and then 15 days later?

00:01:21.386 --> 00:01:22.433
(audience laughs)

00:01:22.433 --> 00:01:23.790
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:23.790 --> 00:01:25.950
- I am on my couch.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:25.950 --> 00:01:27.780
There's no hiking, there's no nothing,

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.760
and the shoes are just
sitting by my front door,

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:30.593
waiting to be used.

00:01:30.593 --> 00:01:31.950
- You're from the most
beautiful place ever.

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:33.033
Canada's so beautiful to hike.

00:01:33.033 --> 00:01:34.396
- It is very, yeah.
- Yeah.

00:01:34.396 --> 00:01:35.506
- Yeah, didn't do that in Canada.

00:01:35.506 --> 00:01:36.677
- Especially British Columbia.

00:01:36.677 --> 00:01:37.637
(audience laughs)

00:01:37.637 --> 00:01:39.990
Just a hard no, I love it.

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:41.610
So wait, you and your
dad are both part of,

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:43.680
it's called the Order of Canada, right?

00:01:43.680 --> 00:01:44.670
I've talked about this with your dad.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:45.803
You're now both a part of it.
- Yeah.

00:01:45.803 --> 00:01:47.730
- This is a big deal in Canada.

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:49.290
- It is, it's like, I don't, it's like,

00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:50.670
I don't know, am I a knight now?

00:01:50.670 --> 00:01:51.683
Like, I don't know what that is.

00:01:51.683 --> 00:01:52.530
- I think so.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:54.180
- I'm gonna just be knighted, I guess,

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:55.020
is what it comes down to.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:56.700
You know, it's like a great honor.

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:58.500
I will say, yes.

00:01:58.500 --> 00:01:59.550
It's like a tremendous honor,

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:01.809
and I don't wanna discredit
Canada, obviously.

00:02:01.809 --> 00:02:02.642
- [Kelly] No.

00:02:02.642 --> 00:02:03.900
- But my dad has been
on here so many times

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:05.910
that I feel like that
has been the great honor.

00:02:05.910 --> 00:02:07.962
And now it's my, on top of like-

00:02:07.962 --> 00:02:08.795
- [Kelly] I love your dad.

00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:09.738
- I should be wearing my pin, actually.

00:02:09.738 --> 00:02:11.679
- Aw, I love your dad.

00:02:11.679 --> 00:02:13.583
- He loves you so much.

00:02:13.583 --> 00:02:15.210
- I, ditto.

00:02:15.210 --> 00:02:16.043
I love him so much.

00:02:16.043 --> 00:02:17.070
- That is the great honor.

00:02:17.070 --> 00:02:21.120
I mean, you know, a country
recognizing you is great,

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:23.487
but being here is also great.

00:02:23.487 --> 00:02:24.864
- Aw, I love, has anybody,

00:02:24.864 --> 00:02:27.480
is there ever in the Order of Canada,

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.280
is there like a father-son
or a mother-daughter?

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:30.450
- I actually have no idea.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:31.530
- I think that you might be the first.

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:32.363
I'm just saying it.

00:02:32.363 --> 00:02:33.196
- [Dan] Let's just say we're the first.

00:02:33.196 --> 00:02:34.785
- Let's just say it, yeah.
- I think we should.

00:02:34.785 --> 00:02:36.100
- Breaking glass ceilings.
(audience cheers)

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:36.959
I like it, I like it.

00:02:36.959 --> 00:02:38.577
- I think you get another
one when that happens.

00:02:38.577 --> 00:02:39.990
- [Kelly] Yes, I think you're just

00:02:39.990 --> 00:02:41.610
a special, special member.

00:02:41.610 --> 00:02:42.443
You get another pin.

00:02:42.443 --> 00:02:44.550
- A Guinness record sort
of right at the end,

00:02:44.550 --> 00:02:46.140
just on the bottom page.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:47.970
- Well, wait, speaking of Dan's dad,

00:02:47.970 --> 00:02:50.670
he told a story about Dan the
first time he was at our show.

00:02:50.670 --> 00:02:51.503
So let's see that.

00:02:51.503 --> 00:02:52.336
- Uh-oh.
- Yeah.

00:02:52.336 --> 00:02:54.180
- My son was 15,

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:59.180
and he was going to come
with us in Los Angeles.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:01.120
We went down for the premiere.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.240
- Yeah.
- Of the movie.

00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:05.213
And the night before the premiere,

00:03:05.213 --> 00:03:08.160
he said he was on the phone call

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:10.410
with his friend back in Toronto.

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:11.970
And they said, "Well, what's going on?"

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:14.972
He said, "Well, I'm going to
the premiere of 'American Pie'

00:03:14.972 --> 00:03:16.830
tomorrow night with my folks."

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:21.092
And the friend said, "Oh,
don't see it with your folks!"

00:03:21.092 --> 00:03:23.556
(audience laughs)

00:03:23.556 --> 00:03:25.249
- Did you not, did you not go?

00:03:25.249 --> 00:03:26.082
- No!

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:28.183
Absolutely not!

00:03:28.183 --> 00:03:29.880
- Oh my god.
- Absolutely not.

00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:30.930
No, I called a friend.

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:33.240
They had just seen like an
advanced screening of it,

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:36.180
and said whatever, run
in the opposite direction

00:03:36.180 --> 00:03:39.330
of wherever your parents are
in relation to that film.

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:40.163
And I did.

00:03:40.163 --> 00:03:42.660
And I never, I have yet
to see it with them.

00:03:42.660 --> 00:03:44.220
I did end up seeing it.

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:46.290
For most of my high school life,

00:03:46.290 --> 00:03:49.089
people thought the movie was
like a biography of my life.

00:03:49.089 --> 00:03:50.908
- Yeah.
(audience laughs)

00:03:50.908 --> 00:03:53.370
That is unfortunate.

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:55.027
- Which is really unfortunate.

00:03:55.027 --> 00:03:55.860
- That's unfortunate.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:56.693
- Because I would've killed

00:03:56.693 --> 00:03:57.980
to have had a life that interesting.

00:03:57.980 --> 00:03:59.100
(audience laughs)

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:00.600
- And why apple pie?
- There was no pies.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:01.433
- Yeah.

00:04:01.433 --> 00:04:03.270
- I don't even know about the
ingredients of an apple pie,

00:04:03.270 --> 00:04:04.844
but that's a whole other conversation.

00:04:04.844 --> 00:04:06.390
(Kelly laughs)
(audience laughs)

00:04:06.390 --> 00:04:08.100
- My, I think my mother's the opposite.

00:04:08.100 --> 00:04:09.840
I would be fine, I
think, in that scenario.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:11.490
I think my mother would die.

00:04:11.490 --> 00:04:12.960
- Okay, you would be fine.
- I'm very open.

00:04:12.960 --> 00:04:13.980
I'm very open.

00:04:13.980 --> 00:04:16.920
I will talk to my mother about anything.

00:04:16.920 --> 00:04:19.650
So will my sister, and my mother hates it.

00:04:19.650 --> 00:04:20.520
- Wow!
(audience laughs)

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:22.170
- Like with a capital H.

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:23.070
- Okay.
- Yeah.

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:24.742
- I don't have that dynamic.

00:04:24.742 --> 00:04:27.586
My dad is very sweet,
but no, there was no-

00:04:27.586 --> 00:04:28.860
- I have no problem.

00:04:28.860 --> 00:04:30.180
I could talk about anything with her,

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:32.700
like literally anything, and I try,

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:34.500
but she is just not, she is like,

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:36.000
why are, your mouth is moving-

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:38.220
- She's the opposite of
what this movie was to me.

00:04:38.220 --> 00:04:40.080
- Yeah, she's not into it,

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:41.970
so I would've gone to the premiere.

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:44.550
Anyway, your dad's in a
(bleep) cool, awesome movie.

00:04:44.550 --> 00:04:45.480
I would've gone.

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:47.460
- Yeah, you know, it's a weird thing

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:49.680
to grow up watching your
dad just hand out porn

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:50.873
to some other kid.

00:04:50.873 --> 00:04:52.367
(Kelly laughs)
(audience laughs)

00:04:52.367 --> 00:04:54.698
(audience applauds)

00:04:54.698 --> 00:04:56.790
- That was a scene from
his movie, "Good Grief."

00:04:56.790 --> 00:04:58.290
You can see it now on Netflix.

00:04:58.290 --> 00:05:00.480
So, explain the movie for everybody.

00:05:00.480 --> 00:05:04.999
- Oh, it's like, it's kind
of a 180 from my TV show.

00:05:04.999 --> 00:05:05.832
- That's what I was gonna say.

00:05:05.832 --> 00:05:07.770
Did you purposely kind of
do a departure from comedy?

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:10.440
- Yeah, I mean, I was never like a comic.

00:05:10.440 --> 00:05:11.580
I wasn't a standup, I didn't,

00:05:11.580 --> 00:05:13.650
I just happened to write a funny show,

00:05:13.650 --> 00:05:15.420
and play sort of a funny character.

00:05:15.420 --> 00:05:16.890
- You have great comedic timing,

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:18.720
so I disagree with that statement.

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:21.330
- I just, yeah, so I
think there was a desire

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:23.970
after 80 episodes of writing
and performing comedy

00:05:23.970 --> 00:05:25.020
to do something very different.

00:05:25.020 --> 00:05:28.560
So, this is a movie about
grief, and about friendship,

00:05:28.560 --> 00:05:31.080
and my character loses his husband,

00:05:31.080 --> 00:05:33.090
and it's really an exploration of,

00:05:33.090 --> 00:05:34.950
that's the amazing Luke Evans.

00:05:34.950 --> 00:05:35.940
- Yeah.
(audience whistling)

00:05:35.940 --> 00:05:38.502
- Which, yeah, it's like tough to cast.

00:05:38.502 --> 00:05:41.564
(audience laughs)
(Kelly laughs)

00:05:41.564 --> 00:05:43.710
It was a tough casting choice.

00:05:43.710 --> 00:05:47.490
But yeah, it's basically about
how this group of friends

00:05:47.490 --> 00:05:51.090
react in the aftermath of
a very meaningful death.

00:05:51.090 --> 00:05:54.360
And I just haven't seen a lot of movies

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:57.540
where friendship was the
center of the conversation.

00:05:57.540 --> 00:05:59.430
- Yeah.
- And that was the love story.

00:05:59.430 --> 00:06:01.830
It didn't require like, romance.

00:06:01.830 --> 00:06:03.570
- But that is a love.

00:06:03.570 --> 00:06:04.860
- It's the greatest love.

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:07.470
- Oh my gosh, I wouldn't have survived

00:06:07.470 --> 00:06:09.900
several situations in my life
without my friend circle.

00:06:09.900 --> 00:06:10.733
Like, do you have that circle?

00:06:10.733 --> 00:06:11.670
- Yeah, yeah, of course,

00:06:11.670 --> 00:06:13.170
and I just felt like that

00:06:13.170 --> 00:06:15.450
deserved its time in the spotlight.

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:17.130
I feel like so many movies,

00:06:17.130 --> 00:06:20.700
rom-coms, and certain dramas,
it's all about the romance.

00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:21.533
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:21.533 --> 00:06:23.040
- And the friends kind
of sit on the sidelines.

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:25.830
But in life, I feel like,
at least in my life,

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:28.890
my friendship is so much
at the center of my life

00:06:28.890 --> 00:06:31.320
that it just felt like a nice film

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:34.620
to kind of explore the
meaning of friendship.

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:37.860
- Yeah, is it, 'cause there's
a lot of depth to this,

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:38.693
and a lot of grief.

00:06:38.693 --> 00:06:42.600
So like, so is that hard for
you to do, like on camera?

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:45.210
- Yeah, my dog died five days

00:06:45.210 --> 00:06:47.124
before I wrote the script for this movie.

00:06:47.124 --> 00:06:47.957
(audience awws)
- Oh no!

00:06:47.957 --> 00:06:50.010
- Yeah, and he was getting old,

00:06:50.010 --> 00:06:51.720
and I knew that I was making this movie.

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:53.520
And so for like, the
year leading up to it,

00:06:53.520 --> 00:06:55.320
I would like sometimes
catch him on my bed,

00:06:55.320 --> 00:06:56.820
and be like, don't you dare.

00:06:56.820 --> 00:06:59.790
It's too sad to like, go
through it without you.

00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:02.880
And then he, yeah, he
passed away five days

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:05.190
before I started, got the
green light for the script.

00:07:05.190 --> 00:07:06.660
And I think there's sometimes

00:07:06.660 --> 00:07:09.450
where things happen in
your life for a reason.

00:07:09.450 --> 00:07:11.760
And I don't know if I'd be
able to write the script

00:07:11.760 --> 00:07:13.590
for this movie with
the same level of like,

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:16.920
vulnerability, or the same
rawness if it hadn't happened.

00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:19.920
And so it's a, I feel very fortunate

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:20.820
to have made something

00:07:20.820 --> 00:07:24.270
that is a celebration
of my feelings for him.

00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:25.860
I lost my grandmother a year before that,

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:28.590
so it was a kind of a catharsis of feeling

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:29.550
when it came to her.

00:07:29.550 --> 00:07:30.690
It has nothing to do with them,

00:07:30.690 --> 00:07:32.682
but it has everything
to do with the feeling.

00:07:32.682 --> 00:07:35.010
- Yeah, and it's a hard feeling,

00:07:35.010 --> 00:07:37.200
'cause I think we
compartmentalize a lot, you know?

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:38.880
You compartmentalize what's going on,

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:41.070
and then all of a sudden
it'll randomly hit you.

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:42.690
- Yeah, and grief is this weird thing

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:44.880
where I feel like everyone

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:47.610
is going through grief of some kind.

00:07:47.610 --> 00:07:49.110
And I think while you're going through it,

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:50.910
you can feel very isolated by it.

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:51.743
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:07:51.743 --> 00:07:52.576
- Whether you're doing it right,

00:07:52.576 --> 00:07:53.820
whether you, whether
there's an appropriate way

00:07:53.820 --> 00:07:55.022
to grieve something.

00:07:55.022 --> 00:07:55.855
- Or length of time.

00:07:55.855 --> 00:07:57.510
- Or length of time, again,

00:07:57.510 --> 00:07:59.400
and so, this movie is really kind of

00:07:59.400 --> 00:08:02.790
explores the idea of that,
and what grief really means,

00:08:02.790 --> 00:08:04.290
and is there a way to do it properly?

00:08:04.290 --> 00:08:07.810
So, it was my way of
kind of working through

00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:10.200
1,000 questions that I
had going on in my head.

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:11.190
- That's the best art though,

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:12.780
when someone's actually
in that raw moment,

00:08:12.780 --> 00:08:15.663
and what comes out of that is you could,

00:08:16.710 --> 00:08:18.658
it's transparent when you're the viewer.

00:08:18.658 --> 00:08:20.880
So, oh, I heard this about
your grandma, actually.

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:22.410
She loved your first story?

00:08:22.410 --> 00:08:23.730
Your very first story that you wrote?

00:08:23.730 --> 00:08:25.740
- Oh yeah, yeah, there
was a, I wrote a story

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:30.453
about a young princess
that traveled to Russia.

00:08:31.320 --> 00:08:34.800
It was a very epic
story that was not good.

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:37.980
And yet, I remember her
sort of pulling me aside

00:08:37.980 --> 00:08:39.720
saying that she was very moved by it.

00:08:39.720 --> 00:08:40.770
She was very touched by it.

00:08:40.770 --> 00:08:42.060
And that, I think, is true love

00:08:42.060 --> 00:08:44.760
when you can read something terrible,

00:08:44.760 --> 00:08:48.030
and really, I think genuinely
convince your grandchild

00:08:48.030 --> 00:08:50.142
